WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.709
Okay.

00:00:00.709 --> 00:00:04.004
So as far as the names Zack and Zoë Sweet
Bee Farm -

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:13.596
Zack and Zoe.

00:00:15.098 --> 00:00:19.227
So we found out
that Zachary had allergies.

00:00:19.227 --> 00:00:24.190
We were in Brooklyn visiting my husband's
sister in Williamsburg,

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:25.650
and we ordered Thai food.

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:28.236
We found out that they had tree
nut and peanut allergies

00:00:29.404 --> 00:00:30.655
the hard way.

00:00:30.864 --> 00:00:33.241
When it comes to seasonal allergies,
just growing up...

00:00:33.241 --> 00:00:35.285
it wasn't like a huge detriment,
but it was just rough

00:00:35.285 --> 00:00:37.871
just in the spring, always
just like having to stay inside, sorta

00:00:37.912 --> 00:00:38.913
just hunker down.

00:00:38.913 --> 00:00:40.999
Couldn’t play as much like sports
and things that I wanted to.

00:00:40.999 --> 00:00:43.501
But honey just kind of change all that.

00:00:43.501 --> 00:00:48.548
The honey is acting
as an anti-inflammatory agent and calming

00:00:48.548 --> 00:01:03.688
all the inflammation down
at the same time.

00:01:03.688 --> 00:01:04.647
It was really my dad's idea

00:01:04.647 --> 00:01:07.984
to start actually keeping bees
and just having them in the backyard.

00:01:07.984 --> 00:01:09.861
At first it was just like
we were buying them.

00:01:09.861 --> 00:01:11.863
My dad has a mentor that we used
to get honey from all the time,

00:01:11.863 --> 00:01:13.865
just like hey, and just switched
gears like,

00:01:13.865 --> 00:01:15.617
this could be our own thing,
we can keep our own bees.

00:01:15.617 --> 00:01:17.035
So we got some bees from him.

00:01:17.035 --> 00:01:18.328
Just set up in the backyard,

00:01:18.328 --> 00:01:22.373
and it's just been having more
and more hives ever since.

00:01:22.373 --> 00:01:25.001
Thought it would be exciting
because living out here,

00:01:25.001 --> 00:01:32.592
you get a real opportunity
to like build your own kind of farm.

00:01:32.592 --> 00:01:36.930
When we started beekeeping, we had no idea
that there would be so many,

00:01:36.971 --> 00:01:40.058
or so much honey, we really got an excess.

00:01:40.058 --> 00:01:44.020
So we gave it out to friends and family
and then we said, you know what, next year

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:45.688
let's start a little business
with the children.

00:01:45.688 --> 00:01:49.400
They'll learn how to have a business
and the ins and outs of that

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:53.113
since Dad's a business man,
we got these little labels going.

00:01:53.196 --> 00:01:55.907
Started to sell it online a bit.

00:01:55.907 --> 00:01:59.160
Eventually we thought,
Well, let's make it a little more serious.

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:03.581
Ended up being really, really a good idea.

00:02:03.706 --> 00:02:06.751
We went to some artisanal markets
and started to sell the honey

00:02:07.127 --> 00:02:16.302
and it really took off.

00:02:16.302 --> 00:02:18.346
Is that small enough? I hope so.

00:02:18.346 --> 00:02:20.682
It doesn’t have to be a full thing.

00:02:20.682 --> 00:02:26.271
Stuff this....lot of smoky stuff.

00:02:26.271 --> 00:02:27.897
That's not...

00:02:27.897 --> 00:02:28.982
Okay. Yeah.

00:02:28.982 --> 00:02:32.026
Okay. Oh, see, how it...

00:02:32.026 --> 00:02:33.111
Yeah, that's perfect.

00:02:33.111 --> 00:02:33.653
Yeah.

00:02:33.653 --> 00:02:36.281
That's actually a perfect like,
not too much.

00:02:36.281 --> 00:02:44.080
Not too little.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.499
So when you're out beekeeping,
you need four things.

00:02:46.499 --> 00:02:49.335
You need your bee suit,
you need a hive tool,

00:02:49.544 --> 00:02:51.880
you need the smoker
and you need the bee brush.

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:56.301
Basically, the smoker tricks
the bees into thinking there's a fire,

00:02:56.342 --> 00:03:00.221
so they're more focused on that situation
than you going into the hive

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:01.514
and taking the honey.

00:03:01.514 --> 00:03:05.977
We approach the hive as a team,
so one person will usually be smoking

00:03:05.977 --> 00:03:09.314
and the other will use the hive tool
and lift up the beehives.

00:03:09.731 --> 00:03:13.359
And so we definitely need to communicate
with each other, that's one thing.

00:03:13.484 --> 00:03:17.488
If you're beekeeping by yourself, then
make sure you have both of those ready.

00:03:17.655 --> 00:03:29.083
Just be alert
and attentive to what the bees are doing.

00:03:29.083 --> 00:03:30.627
Bees are pollinators, right?

00:03:30.627 --> 00:03:34.547
So bees will typically
they eat pollen from the plant.

00:03:34.547 --> 00:03:37.759
They also eat the nectar
and they go in, ingest that

00:03:37.759 --> 00:03:41.471
with kind of enzymes in their own body
in that they reproduce honey.

00:03:41.512 --> 00:03:43.973
So that honey is a food source
for the bees.

00:03:43.973 --> 00:03:46.184
It's a food source for their young ones.

00:03:46.184 --> 00:03:48.645
They’ll eat the honey,
but they'll also store it and save it

00:03:48.853 --> 00:03:54.108
as their food source for the wintertime.

00:03:54.108 --> 00:03:54.484
I mean, it's

00:03:54.484 --> 00:03:58.571
literally hundreds of types of of honey,
depending on the floral source.

00:03:58.738 --> 00:04:01.366
You've got honey
that's got more of a blueberry honey

00:04:01.366 --> 00:04:05.620
that pollinate the blueberry groves
or the cranberry bogs, even,

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:06.412
you know, honey

00:04:06.412 --> 00:04:10.917
bee pollinate in the California almonds
has a different like a more of a nuttier,

00:04:10.959 --> 00:04:11.918
you know, profile

00:04:11.918 --> 00:04:15.713
than, you know, honey, that is just a
more more of a floral source.

00:04:15.713 --> 00:04:18.466
And so it really is
fascinating to understand

00:04:18.466 --> 00:04:22.303
and just taste the different notes
and to see, you know, all honey is not the

00:04:22.303 --> 00:04:22.720
same.

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:34.148
And so it's really cool
to see people experience it.

00:04:34.148 --> 00:04:38.569
Once you go into the hive, you've taken
off all the protective coverings.

00:04:38.945 --> 00:04:41.864
There's about nine frames in each box.

00:04:41.864 --> 00:04:45.451
And so you're going to remove a frame
to harvest the honey.

00:04:45.868 --> 00:04:48.705
And we're just taking out one
for breakfast tomorrow.

00:04:48.705 --> 00:04:52.583
And so when you take the frame out,
you just make sure that

00:04:53.251 --> 00:04:57.463
there's honey on it, first of all, in
that it's not a frame

00:04:57.463 --> 00:05:00.508
that might have babies in it, brood,
they call it.

00:05:01.175 --> 00:05:03.761
When there's honey,
they'll have little capsules over it.

00:05:03.761 --> 00:05:06.097
So you'll see the same sort
of hexagon pattern

00:05:06.723 --> 00:05:08.433
where there's honey
or whether without honey.

00:05:08.433 --> 00:05:11.311
But when there's no honey there,
they're just not, you won’t

00:05:11.602 --> 00:05:13.813
see the little cap over it,
which is where the honey’s stored.

00:05:14.105 --> 00:05:17.317
So like us, the bees are storing up food
for the winter.

00:05:17.567 --> 00:05:20.194
And here in North America, essentially,

00:05:20.653 --> 00:05:23.698
that's an important part of their day.

00:05:23.698 --> 00:05:27.160
They're thinking about how to make sure
they have enough for the queen,

00:05:27.327 --> 00:05:31.122
the whole hive, the brood,
just to make it through the winter.

00:05:31.122 --> 00:05:32.749
So you don't want to take too much honey

00:05:32.749 --> 00:05:34.959
when you're harvesting,
you want to make sure they have enough

00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:39.130
food to make it through
to the next warm season.

00:05:40.381 --> 00:05:42.467
When you’re brushing the bees off
to get your honey..

00:05:42.467 --> 00:05:45.303
just brush them back into the hive,
you know, just down

00:05:45.345 --> 00:05:46.262
motion and straight back in.

00:05:46.262 --> 00:05:48.139
So they don't have to get like confused
or dazed.

00:05:48.139 --> 00:05:51.434
I'll just be right back where they belong
and just make sure they're none left.

00:05:51.434 --> 00:05:53.770
You don’t want
just like one last bee around your house.

00:05:55.313 --> 00:05:58.441
Yeah, expect one or two to follow
you home.

00:05:59.442 --> 00:06:03.488
Beekeeping is just as easy as it is
that you're brave.

00:06:03.905 --> 00:06:07.617
So if you want, honey,
all you have to do is have some courage

00:06:07.617 --> 00:06:11.662
and enjoy yourself.

00:06:11.662 --> 00:06:13.498
At first, I thought mixing superfoods

00:06:13.498 --> 00:06:16.584
with the honey would be really strange
and it wouldn't really take off.

00:06:16.876 --> 00:06:20.463
But once Ma tried it,
it tasted amazing and everyone

00:06:20.463 --> 00:06:26.552
we sold it to really,
really loved the idea.

00:06:26.552 --> 00:06:32.350
I would go to the organic markets
and when I would look at the shelves

00:06:32.558 --> 00:06:33.976
I was like, How are we going to fit?

00:06:33.976 --> 00:06:35.436
To stand out I just said,

00:06:35.436 --> 00:06:40.191
Well, let's add matcha, let's add,
you know, raspberries, let's add ginger.

00:06:40.191 --> 00:06:42.318
And yeah, lavender is a big one.

00:06:42.944 --> 00:06:45.488
The hot honey just came out last year.

00:06:45.655 --> 00:06:48.282
Oh, cardamom
if you haven't tried the cardamom,

00:06:49.242 --> 00:06:49.951
it's just like...

00:06:49.951 --> 00:06:51.035
by the spoonful.

00:06:51.035 --> 00:06:54.122
But, yeah, there's plenty of options
that you can choose from.

00:06:54.122 --> 00:06:58.000
There's, like 23 right now.

00:06:58.876 --> 00:07:03.339
Once you bring a frame in, there's

00:07:03.339 --> 00:07:06.551
something called an extractor

00:07:08.886 --> 00:07:12.098
and so you have a big barrel,
and then inside

00:07:12.265 --> 00:07:15.852
there's just something that spins
the darn honey right out of there.

00:07:15.935 --> 00:07:20.731
All the honey just goes to the bottom
of the whole machine.

00:07:21.482 --> 00:07:23.609
It goes right into the barrel. With a tap.

00:07:23.818 --> 00:07:26.696
Yeah, with the tap on it and those barrels

00:07:26.696 --> 00:07:31.909
go to our production studio.

00:07:31.909 --> 00:07:38.124
We bring the honey in from the trucks
and we take the lids off, stir it up,

00:07:38.416 --> 00:07:42.462
make sure they're nice and settled
and mixed properly.

00:07:42.962 --> 00:07:45.756
Then we take them
and put them into the reservoir.

00:07:45.965 --> 00:07:49.260
The machine holds the honey
and then it's pumped up

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:52.930
into a larger machine
where the superfoods are added.

00:07:52.930 --> 00:07:59.353
And that's where the magic happens.

00:07:59.353 --> 00:08:03.316
Then just, the employees
come and jar them very easily,

00:08:03.524 --> 00:08:06.819
and we're able to label them
in the labeling machine,

00:08:06.819 --> 00:08:09.405
put them in the box,
and then they're ready to go.

00:08:09.405 --> 00:08:13.576
So everything you get
is just really literally that honey

00:08:13.576 --> 00:08:15.161
and that organic superfood.

00:08:15.161 --> 00:08:22.084
And that's the only process really
that we have going there.

00:08:22.084 --> 00:08:26.464
So a lot of the honey
goes out to direct to consumer, others

00:08:26.464 --> 00:08:31.219
go to larger retailers,
but a portion of it goes to Chelsea Market

00:08:31.219 --> 00:08:40.478
where we're able to sell it face to face.

00:08:40.478 --> 00:08:43.606
The best part about Chelsea
is just everything,

00:08:43.606 --> 00:08:46.692
but the neighborhood is
what we were so excited about.

00:08:46.692 --> 00:08:50.863
And having a spot
there is like has been epic for us.

00:08:50.863 --> 00:08:55.409
Interacting with the customers, people
all over the world, an amazing experience.

00:08:55.409 --> 00:08:57.411
So we bring in the honey as a family.

00:08:57.411 --> 00:08:59.455
We have a good time with it.

00:08:59.455 --> 00:09:03.167
So and we have an amazing team
that works there too.

00:09:03.459 --> 00:09:06.879
People have said to us like your,
you know, workers are so sweet

00:09:07.838 --> 00:09:09.298
and I’m like it’s the honey.

00:09:09.298 --> 00:09:16.055
You guys, this is Zach and Zoë,
from Zach and Zoë’s farm.

00:09:16.055 --> 00:09:16.138
Made my day!
You guys are cuter than your picture.

00:09:16.556 --> 00:09:20.059
After that,
we just make sure that the customers

00:09:20.059 --> 00:09:24.480
are satisfied
and everything is running smoothly.

00:09:24.855 --> 00:09:27.942
And at the end of the day,
if everyone has their honey,

00:09:27.942 --> 00:09:40.621
then we're good to go.

00:09:40.621 --> 00:09:43.541
The kids have literally grown up
with bees, and so, it's fun

00:09:43.541 --> 00:09:48.879
because as they get older, I mean, Zach is
14, Zoë is 15, going to be 16 next week.

00:09:48.879 --> 00:09:52.008
As they get more independent,
you miss the moments, and the times

00:09:52.174 --> 00:09:54.176
you spent with them.
And so it's a little, little bittersweet.

00:09:54.176 --> 00:09:57.805
I mean, you're you're happy to see your
kids grow up, but you certainly miss them.

00:09:58.180 --> 00:10:03.144
And you miss the memories
and you miss them putting on the bee

00:10:03.144 --> 00:10:07.565
suits, or holding a smoker for dad
or bringing me the the hive tool.

00:10:07.565 --> 00:10:12.862
So, you know, hopefully right,
when they get older and get married,

00:10:13.154 --> 00:10:17.116
their kids want to come spend time
with grandpa doing bee work.

00:10:17.116 --> 00:10:20.661
So we'll see.

00:10:20.661 --> 00:10:24.457
When the business started,
it began to become tied with my upbringing

00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:27.960
and even before I felt like that
we were prepared

00:10:28.210 --> 00:10:31.339
to be raised into this business
and we were equipped with the skills

00:10:31.756 --> 00:10:35.968
to keep the business running forward
even after like mom and dad eventually

00:10:35.968 --> 00:10:38.471
like retire. I don't know.
You don't seem like the type to retire.

00:10:38.554 --> 00:10:39.972
She keeps saying
can say, I'm never going to,

00:10:41.349 --> 00:10:44.435
but yeah, I'm really, really grateful
for that,

00:10:44.435 --> 00:10:47.772
that we have something that has set us up
for some serious

00:10:47.772 --> 00:10:51.359
success. Aww

00:10:51.567 --> 00:10:55.446
It's a lot to have a business with
your name on it, just like a big deal.

00:10:55.446 --> 00:10:56.739
And I like thank Mom and Dad, of course,

00:10:56.739 --> 00:10:57.948
because they did most of the heavy
lifting.

00:10:57.948 --> 00:11:01.118
Obviously, you can't really run a business
when you're seven, but

00:11:01.786 --> 00:11:04.872
it's definitely just a huge thing that’s
like just a kind of a part of me now.

00:11:04.872 --> 00:11:06.540
I like pitch in, help.

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:08.542
Just something on my mind, I’m
always thinking about it,

00:11:08.542 --> 00:11:10.836
what's new and like
what's coming up over the horizon.

00:11:10.836 --> 00:11:11.504
But it's like a

00:11:11.504 --> 00:11:14.715
just a nice state of mind to be in,
just like hey, like this is my own thing.

00:11:14.924 --> 00:11:17.677
And then this is like eventually
I have to just carry on on my own like,

00:11:17.802 --> 00:11:20.805
they can't run the business forever,
so like this is going to be my own thing

00:11:21.138 --> 00:11:25.309
in the future, which is a big deal for me.

